Subject: [PATCH] fix(test): FLOOR-02 - two assertions FLOOR-01 broke, and I
 should have run them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`pnpm test:web`: 921 passed, 2 failed, both in floor-assembly.test.ts, both
caused by FLOOR-01. I ran the new geometry test and the data assertions by hand
and did not re-run this file after changing the texture path and the way the
parquet turn is decomposed. That is on me, and it is the second time in this
series that a file I did not re-run was the file that broke.

Neither failure is a floor that is wrong. Both are assertions that pinned a
DECOMPOSITION which changed, while the thing they exist to protect is intact —
and both come back stronger than they were.

1. 'every species has a photographed crop on disk'

    expected '/textures/grain-white-oak-along.webp'
          to be '/textures/grain-white-oak.webp'

It asserted the portrait crop. FLOOR-01 pointed this surface at the landscape
one, because the build turns every tile so the grain runs DOWN it — right for
the renderer, which samples in board-local inches and turns the grain itself,
and wrong here, where CSS cannot rotate a background and a board carries its
length along the div's width. The portrait tile painted the figure across every
plank.

Now: the along tile must exist, the portrait tile it was cut from must ALSO
still exist, and they must be two different files. A build that stopped writing
the pair, or wrote the same file twice, fails here instead of on the homepage.
One claim became three.

2. 'turn the parquet patterns to 45 degrees and the plank patterns to zero'

    herringbone: expected [0, 90] to deeply equal [-45, 45]

It compared board `rot` alone. That held while both parquet patterns put the
whole turn on each board; FLOOR-01 stopped doing that for herringbone, which is
now generated axis-aligned with the FIELD turned 45° — how it is actually laid,
the pattern square and the room at an angle to it, and the only arrangement in
which the lattice closes exactly.

So the test failed on a floor that is correct, which is the worst kind of
failing test: it argues for putting the defect back.

What a visitor sees is rot PLUS fieldRot. That is what is asserted now,
normalised to a half turn because a board at 135° and a board at −45° are the
same board. Measured:

    straight      rot [0]       field  0  → on screen [0]
    diagonal      rot [0]       field 45  → on screen [45]
    herringbone   rot [0, 90]   field 45  → on screen [45, 135]
    chevron       rot [-45, 45] field  0  → on screen [45, 135]

Both parquet patterns land on the same pair, as they must. The assertion is now
true however the turn is split between board and field, and it would still have
caught the original defect — which is the test for whether a rewritten
assertion is honest or merely convenient.

NO GUARD WAS WEAKENED. Two assertions moved from checking how a value is
represented to checking what it renders as, and both cover more than before.

VERIFIED

  Both rewritten assertions executed here against the built tiles and the real
  generator, with the assertions around them — determinism, fieldRot, scale,
  and straight-plank rotation — re-run alongside.
  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css fails only in this sandbox.

